WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:05.300
Film Courage:&nbsp; You were doing re-writes for
several years.&nbsp; When did you first sell a

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:06.300
script?

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:14.950
Christine Conradt:&nbsp;Gosh…I don’t think
I sold a spec until years into my career?

00:00:14.950 --> 00:00:21.300
Most of my early work was doing rewrites and
then talking to the producers saying “You

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:25.740
are basically paying other people the bulk
of the money to do this original draft and

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:29.550
I’m fixing it. Why don’t you just hire
me to start?”

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:34.379
Then they said “Yes, we will do that.”&nbsp;
But those were still work for hire projects,

00:00:34.379 --> 00:00:37.809
but they were still coming to me with the
concepts and the ideas or whatever and then

00:00:37.809 --> 00:00:39.559
I was just writing them.

00:00:39.559 --> 00:00:45.549
It took a long time before I sold a spec that
I had just written on my own and taken to

00:00:45.549 --> 00:00:46.549
market.

00:00:46.549 --> 00:00:49.409
Film Courage:&nbsp; It sounds like the traditional
way one thinks of a screenwriter going to

00:00:49.409 --> 00:00:53.459
pitch and they [a studio] are going to say
yes and write you a check, you already had

00:00:53.459 --> 00:00:58.719
inroads and were already doing work, but were
fixing others work and then you essentially

00:00:58.719 --> 00:01:00.049
created your own job?
&nbsp;

00:01:00.049 --> 00:01:03.969
Christine Conradt: Yes, basically.&nbsp; Most
people, when they think of screenwriting,

00:01:03.969 --> 00:01:08.909
they think of the pitch and going into the
room and pitching and someone saying “Yes!

00:01:08.909 --> 00:01:13.770
Write this for us. We love it.” And then
you sell it. And that certainly exists. It

00:01:13.770 --> 00:01:20.700
didn’t for me. There’s a ton of work in
rewriting other people’s stuff. And most

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:24.869
writers just don’t know how to rewrite.
They take it to the level that they can, maybe

00:01:24.869 --> 00:01:29.280
they don’t want to change it. They really
fall in love with their stuff and they don’t

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:33.780
want to make any changes and (ultimately)
that makes them get fired off of a project

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:38.509
and then they bring on another writer that
will make the changes. Or they have just taken

00:01:38.509 --> 00:01:45.171
it to as much as they are able to take it
and they want to bring in a fresh sense, so

00:01:45.171 --> 00:01:47.880
sometimes producers will do that.

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:50.859
That for me was where most of my early work
was from.

00:01:50.859 --> 00:01:55.780
Film Courage: I think that’s great, too
because you know the style of the person you

00:01:55.780 --> 00:02:00.140
are going to be writing for…I think that’s
an excellent way…do a lot of people get

00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:03.460
in [land writing jobs] this way? Or is this
an interesting way to create your own job?

00:02:03.460 --> 00:02:07.460
Christine Conradt: I don’t know if a lot
of people get in that way. I know other writers

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:13.210
especially in TV when you are doing features
for television, a lot of writers get in that

00:02:13.210 --> 00:02:20.200
way. Just being able to write other people’s
rewrites. You know that’s key. If you can

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:25.129
do that, you are pretty much going to get
hired again and again because there aren’t

00:02:25.129 --> 00:02:28.270
a lot of people who know how to do that (being
able to really listen to what the producer

00:02:28.270 --> 00:02:32.019
wants). And the more you do this (obviously),
the more you understand budgets, what they

00:02:32.019 --> 00:02:37.250
want, and sometimes when you get cryptic notes
you can sometimes navigate through those notes

00:02:37.250 --> 00:02:39.180
a little bit better.

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:42.790
But that is one way in that a lot of people
don’t think of.

00:02:42.790 --> 00:02:46.739
Film Courage: And I’m sure the writer who
does the rewrites is much easier to deal with

00:02:46.739 --> 00:02:50.459
because they are not as precious, in terms
of it’s not their work, it’s not their

00:02:50.459 --> 00:02:51.459
baby that is being criticized.

00:02:51.459 --> 00:02:56.140
Christine Conradt: That’s very true. Writers
have to be very careful about being precious

00:02:56.140 --> 00:03:01.332
because even with my original stuff, I have
to take a step back and make sure that I am

00:03:01.332 --> 00:03:05.030
not being that way. Because I don’t want
to suddenly become the writer that they want

00:03:05.030 --> 00:03:10.519
to fire and hire someone else. But it’s
difficult you know, especially the more time

00:03:10.519 --> 00:03:16.829
you invest in a project, I feel like the more
precious it becomes to you. Which is why I

00:03:16.829 --> 00:03:22.720
think it’s important to finish projects.
Some writers will work on something for three

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:29.750
or four years, like one script. And you’ve
got to end it and move onto the next thing.

00:03:29.750 --> 00:03:33.260
Because every time you move onto that next
thing, you become a better writer. So get

00:03:33.260 --> 00:03:39.140
those first seven under your belt and click
them off and then keep moving and you can

00:03:39.140 --> 00:03:41.860
always revisit them. But you’ve got to keep
going through it.

00:03:41.860 --> 00:03:46.870
Film Courage: So even though the way you sold
your first script through, the fact that you

00:03:46.870 --> 00:03:51.640
were already doing rewrites and people already
knew your writing and loved what you did,

00:03:51.640 --> 00:03:55.830
do you then go in cold and is that a way that
you’ve pitched?

00:03:55.830 --> 00:04:01.000
Christine Conradt: I’ve never sold anything
that way, ever. And I’ve done it. Just like

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:08.079
every writer, I’ve gone into the room and
pitched. But typically those projects don’t

00:04:08.079 --> 00:04:12.709
pan out, at least they haven’t for me. Most
of the projects I get are because you’ve

00:04:12.709 --> 00:04:16.800
met a director, you’ve met an actor, you’ve
met a producer, you’ve talked about it for

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:20.920
awhile “Yeah, we want to work together.”
You come up with an idea, is this the right

00:04:20.920 --> 00:04:26.460
time? Eventually everything aligns and they
say “Hey, we’ve got the money. Do you

00:04:26.460 --> 00:04:29.640
want to do this script? Let’s go for it
now.” And that’s how it works.

